ML_PAGE("t4-prep", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l4\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">TOPIC 4 · MCQ PREPARATION</span><h2>Topic 4 MCQ Prep</h2><p>The complete MCQ preparation notes in their original order: 45 multiple-choice questions sit inside the sections they belong to, with answers hidden until you reveal them.</p></header><div class=\"study-grid three-col\"><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>45</strong><span>MCQs in the notes</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>45</strong><span>With worked reasoning</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>Difficult-mode questions</span></div></div><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l4\" data-quiz=\"4\">Start Topic 4 simulator</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l4\" id=\"t4p-toc\"><summary><strong>MCQ notes</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">15 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li class=\"toc-group\">PART A - HIGH-YIELD CONCEPTS</li><li data-full=\"Part 1 - High-yield concepts you must identify instantly\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4p-s2\" title=\"Part 1 - High-yield concepts you must identify instantly\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P1</span>High-yield concepts you must identify instantly</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 2 - Classifying E, S and G correctly\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4p-s3\" title=\"Part 2 - Classifying E, S and G correctly\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P2</span>Classifying E, S and G correctly</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 3 - ESG risks vs ordinary enterprise risks\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4p-s4\" title=\"Part 3 - ESG risks vs ordinary enterprise risks\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P3</span>ESG risks vs ordinary enterprise risks</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 4 - ESG risk materiality\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4p-s5\" title=\"Part 4 - ESG risk materiality\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P4</span>ESG risk materiality</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 5 - ESG risk exposure vs risk management quality\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4p-s6\" title=\"Part 5 - ESG risk exposure vs risk management quality\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P5</span>ESG risk exposure vs risk management quality</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART B - GREENWASHING, REPORTING &amp; FINANCE</li><li data-full=\"Part 6 - Greenwashing\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4p-s8\" title=\"Part 6 - Greenwashing\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P6</span>Greenwashing</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 7 - ESG reporting standards\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4p-s9\" title=\"Part 7 - ESG reporting standards\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P7</span>ESG reporting standards</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 8 - Green finance vs green investing\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4p-s10\" title=\"Part 8 - Green finance vs green investing\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P8</span>Green finance vs green investing</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 9 - Malaysian ESG facts and examples\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4p-s11\" title=\"Part 9 - Malaysian ESG facts and examples\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P9</span>Malaysian ESG facts and examples</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 10 - The fast MCQ recognition sheet\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4p-s12\" title=\"Part 10 - The fast MCQ recognition sheet\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P10</span>The fast MCQ recognition sheet</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART C - MOCK EXAM</li><li data-full=\"Part 11 - Mock MCQ exam\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4p-s14\" title=\"Part 11 - Mock MCQ exam\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P11</span>Mock MCQ exam</button></li><li data-full=\"Mock exam - answers and reasoning\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4p-s15\" title=\"Mock exam - answers and reasoning\">Mock exam - answers and reasoning</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART D - DIFFICULT QUESTIONS &amp; FINAL REVIEW</li><li data-full=\"Part 12 - How to think through difficult questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4p-s17\" title=\"Part 12 - How to think through difficult questions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P12</span>How to think through difficult questions</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 13 - What to memorise the night before\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4p-s18\" title=\"Part 13 - What to memorise the night before\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P13</span>What to memorise the night before</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 14 - Final exam strategy\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4p-s19\" title=\"Part 14 - Final exam strategy\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P14</span>Final exam strategy</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"prep-notes theme-l4\" open><summary><div><strong>Topic 4 MCQ preparation</strong><small>Distinctions, traps and practice questions, section by section, answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body compact\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>15 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><p>For Topic 4, the biggest danger in MCQs is confusing concepts that sound similar but operate at different levels.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Sustainability} \\neq \\text{ESG} \\neq \\text{SDGs}$$</div>\n<p>Similarly:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{ESG Performance} \\neq \\text{ESG Rating}$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Environmental Risk} \\neq \\text{Environmental Impact}$$</div>\n<p>This chapter covers sustainability, sustainable development, corporate sustainability, reporting standards, ESG risk management, greenwashing, green financing, Malaysian ESG practices and the SDGs.</p>\n<p>I'll prepare you using the same approach as Topics 1-3, moving from concepts to difficult scenario-based MCQs.</p>\n<h3>The exam strategy</h3>\n<p>Use these six questions when you encounter an unfamiliar MCQ.</p>\n<p>Is the question about a global goal, a business practice, or a measurement?</p>\n<p>Is the underlying issue environmental, social or governance-related?</p>\n<p>Is it asking about the root cause or the business consequence?</p>\n<p>Is the company improving actual performance or merely reporting a score?</p>\n<p>Is the decision about short-term profit or long-term sustainable value?</p>\n<p>What is the material risk, and which control would address it?</p><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"t4p-d1\">PART A - HIGH-YIELD CONCEPTS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4p-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 1 - High-yield concepts you must identify instantly</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t4_concept_sorter\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Eight things called &quot;sustainability&quot;</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Match each statement to the concept it describes, then reveal.</figcaption></figure></div><h3>1. Sustainability vs sustainable development</h3>\n<p>The chapter defines sustainability around meeting present needs without compromising future generations, while balancing economic growth, environmental care and social well-being.</p>\n<p>For MCQs, think:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Present needs + Future needs</span></div>\n<p>Trap: Sustainability is not exclusively environmental protection.</p>\n<p>If an option includes long-term economic, social and environmental balance, it is more complete than one focusing only on planting trees or recycling.</p>\n<h3>2. Triple Bottom Line - 3Ps</h3>\n<p>People</p>\n<p>Social</p>\n<p>Planet</p>\n<p>Environmental</p>\n<p>Profit</p>\n<p>Economic</p>\n<p>The lecture associates the Triple Bottom Line with John Elkington and the integration of economic, environmental and societal dimensions into corporate sustainability.</p>\n<p>Common trap: Higher profit alone does not establish that a company is sustainable.</p>\n<h3>3. The evolution of sustainability</h3>\n<p>These dates and concepts are worth memorising because they are easy factual MCQ targets.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Date</th>\n<th>Event</th>\n<th>Key distinction</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1987</td>\n<td>Brundtland Commission</td>\n<td>Present and future generations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1992</td>\n<td>UN Conference on Environment and Development</td>\n<td>Development and social actors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1998</td>\n<td>Triple Bottom Line in the lecture timeline</td>\n<td>People, Planet, Profit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2000</td>\n<td>Millennium Development Goals</td>\n<td>8 goals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2015</td>\n<td>Sustainable Development Goals</td>\n<td>17 goals, 169 targets</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These are the dates as organised in your lecture's sustainability timeline, pages 4-6.</p>\n<h3>4. SDGs - 5Ps</h3>\n<p>The UN 2030 Agenda has:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{17\\text{ Goals} + 169\\text{ Targets}}$$</div>\n<p>Its five overarching themes are:</p>\n<p>People, Planet, Prosperity, Peace, Partnership.</p>\n<p>Trap: Do not confuse the Triple Bottom Line's 3Ps with the SDGs' 5Ps.</p>\n<p>Also remember:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>SDGs are global development goals, not company ESG scores.</span></div>\n<h3>5. Corporate sustainability vs ESG vs SDGs</h3>\n<p>This is probably the single highest-value distinction in the chapter.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Question it answers</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Sustainability</td>\n<td>Can present needs be met without undermining future needs?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Corporate sustainability</td>\n<td>How does a firm operate and create value sustainably?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SDGs</td>\n<td>What global development outcomes should be pursued?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ESG</td>\n<td>What environmental, social and governance factors are relevant to an organisation?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ESG metrics</td>\n<td>How are those factors measured?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ESG risk management</td>\n<td>How are material ESG risks identified, assessed and managed?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>Example MCQ trap</h4>\n<p>\"A company reduces factory emissions to contribute to SDG 13.\"</p>\n<ul>\n<li>SDG 13 is the global goal.</li>\n<li>Emissions reduction is the corporate ESG action.</li>\n<li>Tonnes of CO₂e reduced is a performance metric.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All three relate to sustainability, but they are not the same thing.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4p-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 2 - Classifying E, S and G correctly</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t4_g_backbone\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Without governance, environmental and social commitments have no owner, no verification and no accountability.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The lecture specifies environmental factors such as energy use, emissions and biodiversity; social factors such as labour standards, wages and workplace safety; and governance factors such as board structure, oversight and corruption.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Environmental (E)</th>\n<th>Social (S)</th>\n<th>Governance (G)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>GHG emissions</td>\n<td>Employee health and safety</td>\n<td>Board independence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Water consumption</td>\n<td>Forced labour</td>\n<td>Executive compensation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Deforestation</td>\n<td>Wages and benefits</td>\n<td>Bribery and corruption</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Waste management</td>\n<td>Diversity and inclusion</td>\n<td>Board oversight</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pollution</td>\n<td>Community relationships</td>\n<td>Ethics and compliance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Biodiversity</td>\n<td>Customer data privacy</td>\n<td>Conflicts of interest</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The advanced trap: One incident can involve multiple ESG dimensions.</p>\n<p>Suppose a factory illegally dumps toxic waste.</p>\n<p>The pollution is primarily an E issue.</p>\n<p>If directors deliberately conceal the pollution, there is also a G issue.</p>\n<p>If surrounding communities develop health problems, there are S consequences.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>One event can contain E + S + G dimensions</span></div>\n<p>For an MCQ, identify whether the examiner asks for the primary cause, the immediate impact or the governance weakness.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4p-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 3 - ESG risks vs ordinary enterprise risks</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t4_esg_two_way\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Impact (company on environment) and exposure (environment on company) are both part of ESG risk.</figcaption></figure></div><p>ESG risks can transmit into familiar categories from Topic 1.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>ESG issue</th>\n<th>Transmission</th>\n<th>Business consequence</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Extreme weather</td>\n<td>Factory floods</td>\n<td>Operational and financial risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Forced labour allegations</td>\n<td>Imports restricted</td>\n<td>Supply-chain and business risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Corruption</td>\n<td>Regulatory investigation</td>\n<td>Legal and reputational risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Excessive emissions</td>\n<td>Compliance costs increase</td>\n<td>Financial and strategic risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weak worker safety</td>\n<td>Serious accident</td>\n<td>Operational, legal and reputation risk</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Forced Labour → Trade Restrictions → Lost Revenue\"><span>Forced Labour</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Trade Restrictions</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Lost Revenue</span></div>\n<p>The first problem is a Social ESG issue. Lost revenue is its financial consequence.</p>\n<p>Do not confuse them.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4p-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 4 - ESG risk materiality</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t4_materiality\"><div class=\"vis-title\">ESG materiality by industry</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Choose an industry; its material E, S and G issues light up. Materiality is business-specific.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The lecture describes ESG risk in terms of environmental, social or governance events or conditions capable of materially affecting investment value.</p>\n<p>The critical word is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Material</span></div>\n<p>An ESG factor is financially material when it could meaningfully affect the organisation's performance, prospects or value.</p>\n<p>For example, water scarcity may be especially material for a water-intensive manufacturer, whereas customer data protection may be especially important for a digital financial-services firm.</p>\n<p>Trap: ESG risk management does not mean every ESG issue must be given equal priority.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4p-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 5 - ESG risk exposure vs risk management quality</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Page 41 compares ESG risk exposure, unmanaged ESG risk and management scores across ASEAN and comparison regions.</p>\n<p>These are distinct concepts.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Exposure} \\neq \\text{Management Effectiveness}}$$</div>\n<p>Imagine:</p>\n<p>Company A has high environmental exposure but excellent controls.</p>\n<p>Company B has moderate environmental exposure but almost no controls.</p>\n<p>You cannot infer B is safer merely because its starting exposure is lower.</p>\n<p>The underlying lesson is to assess both what a company faces and how effectively it manages it.</p></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"t4p-d7\">PART B - GREENWASHING, REPORTING &amp; FINANCE</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4p-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 6 - Greenwashing</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t4_greenwash_gap\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Greenwashing = claim greater than evidence. The gap is where credibility risk lives.</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t4_greenwash_detector\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Claim versus evidence</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Set how bold the public claim is, add evidence, and read the gap.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Your lecture describes greenwashing as enhancing an environmentally friendly image without substantive underlying changes.</p>\n<p>For MCQs:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Environmental Claim} &gt; \\text{Supported Reality}}$$</div>\n<p>A classic example is a company promoting a carbon-neutral image without credible supporting information.</p>\n<h4>High-value distinction</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Weak ESG performance: The company actually performs poorly.</li>\n<li>Greenwashing: Sustainability communication is misleading or unsupported.</li>\n<li>ESG reporting: Disclosure of information about ESG performance.</li>\n<li>ESG improvement: Actual changes in operating practices or outcomes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A company can perform poorly and disclose that accurately. Poor performance alone does not automatically constitute greenwashing.</p>\n<p>Conversely, a company may have some legitimate green initiatives while making exaggerated claims about its overall footprint.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4p-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 7 - ESG reporting standards</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t4_reporting_vs_rm\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>A credible report sits on governance, measurement and controls; a pretty one can sit on nothing.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The lecture introduces GRI, ISO-related standards and the ISSB/IFRS sustainability-reporting landscape.</p>\n<p>The essential MCQ distinction is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Reporting} \\neq \\text{Risk Management}}$$</div>\n<p>Reporting communicates information about performance.</p>\n<p>Risk management identifies, evaluates and responds to underlying risks.</p>\n<p>A company can produce a beautifully designed sustainability report and still have weak controls.</p>\n<h4>Reporting frameworks - simple memory model</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Framework/body</th>\n<th>Main association</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>GRI</td>\n<td>Sustainability impact reporting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ISSB</td>\n<td>International sustainability-related financial disclosures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IFRS S1/S2</td>\n<td>General sustainability-related financial information and climate-related disclosure standards, respectively</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ISO</td>\n<td>Standards and guidance for management-related subjects</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The lecture particularly highlights the ISSB and Malaysia's movement toward sustainability reporting requirements. These are useful conceptual associations; the deck does not teach the detailed disclosure clauses of each standard.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4p-s10\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 8 - Green finance vs green investing</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture distinguishes financing environmentally beneficial activities from allocating investments to environmentally oriented companies or projects.</p>\n<p>For MCQs:</p>\n<p>Green financing: Think about raising or providing funding for environmentally focused activities.</p>\n<p>Green investing: Think about an investor allocating capital to environmentally oriented assets or businesses.</p>\n<p>They overlap considerably. The distinction is usually the financing activity versus the investor's allocation perspective.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4p-s11\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 9 - Malaysian ESG facts and examples</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t4_net_zero\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Net zero = gross emissions minus removals equals zero; it needs a measured baseline.</figcaption></figure></div><p>These are some of the most likely source-specific factual questions.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Topic</th>\n<th>What the lecture highlights</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>FTSE4Good Bursa Malaysia</td>\n<td>ESG-related equity index launched in December 2014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Index universe</td>\n<td>Drawn from FTSE Bursa Malaysia EMAS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PETRONAS</td>\n<td>2021 ESG-index exclusion example</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Malaysian glove/palm sectors</td>\n<td>Forced-labour allegations and US import restrictions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sapura Energy</td>\n<td>Executive remuneration/governance concerns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Serba Dinamik</td>\n<td>Financial-reporting/governance case</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ASEAN sustainable bonds and sukuk</td>\n<td>Growth illustrated over 2016-2021</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Malaysia sustainability policy</td>\n<td>Carbon-neutrality and energy-transition discussion</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The examples come from pages 16-22 and 31-44 of your lecture. The numerical company counts, historic chart values and policy snapshots are lecture-period information, not figures you should assume remain current in 2026.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4p-s12\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 10 - The fast MCQ recognition sheet</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t4_concept_map\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>From the idea of sustainability to enterprise value, and how E, S and G migrate into familiar risks.</figcaption></figure></div><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question clue</th>\n<th>Most likely answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Current generation without harming future generations</td>\n<td>Sustainability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>People + Planet + Profit</td>\n<td>Triple Bottom Line</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>People + Planet + Prosperity + Peace + Partnership</td>\n<td>SDG 5Ps</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>17 goals and 169 targets</td>\n<td>SDGs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Company's long-term sustainable strategy</td>\n<td>Corporate sustainability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Climate, energy, biodiversity</td>\n<td>Environmental</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Labour, wages, employee safety</td>\n<td>Social</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Board, corruption, executive pay</td>\n<td>Governance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ESG factors significantly affecting investment value</td>\n<td>ESG risk / materiality</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Exaggerated environmental claims</td>\n<td>Greenwashing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Actual emissions measured in CO₂e</td>\n<td>ESG metric</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Disclosure of ESG performance</td>\n<td>ESG reporting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Indicators warning before adverse events</td>\n<td>ESG risk monitoring</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Financing green projects</td>\n<td>Green finance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Investor choosing environmentally oriented assets</td>\n<td>Green investing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ESG-focused Malaysian equity index</td>\n<td>FTSE4Good Bursa Malaysia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk management integrated with ESG</td>\n<td>Holistic ERM</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Now the important part: testing whether you can apply these rules when the answer is not obvious.</p></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"t4p-d13\">PART C - MOCK EXAM</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4p-s14\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 11 - Mock MCQ exam</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h3>Topic 4 practice test</h3>\n<p>45 original exam-style questions based on your lecture</p>\n<p>Choose the best answer for each question. Answers and explanations stay hidden until you finish.</p>\n<h4>Section A - Sustainability fundamentals</h4>\n<p>Questions 1-10 · Knowledge and conceptual understanding</p>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><div><p>Which statement best represents sustainability?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Maximising present economic output regardless of future effects</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Protecting nature by completely stopping economic activity</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Meeting current needs without compromising future generations' needs</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Maximising shareholder wealth through environmental marketing</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p><strong>Meeting current needs without compromising future generations' needs</strong></p>\n<p>The defining feature is intergenerational responsibility combined with economic, social and environmental balance.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><div><p>A company generates high profit by exhausting a non-renewable resource without planning for future productive capacity. What is the main sustainability concern?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The company is not profitable</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Current performance undermines future capacity</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The company is necessarily violating accounting standards</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Any use of natural resources is prohibited</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p><strong>Current performance undermines future capacity</strong></p>\n<p>Sustainability asks whether present activities compromise future needs, not whether current profit is positive.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><div><p>Which three dimensions make up the Triple Bottom Line?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Policy, People, Prosperity</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Profit, Peace, Partnership</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> People, Planet, Peace</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> People, Planet, Profit</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p><strong>People, Planet, Profit</strong></p>\n<p>The Triple Bottom Line combines social, environmental and economic dimensions.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><div><p>Which is NOT one of the five overarching themes of the UN 2030 Agenda?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Performance</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> People</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Peace</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Partnership</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p><strong>Performance</strong></p>\n<p>The 5Ps are People, Planet, Prosperity, Peace and Partnership.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><div><p>Which historical milestone is most closely associated with defining sustainable development in relation to the needs of future generations?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The 2004 Kofi Annan letter</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The launch of FTSE4Good Bursa Malaysia</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The 1987 Brundtland Commission report</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The 2015 adoption of the SDGs</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p><strong>The 1987 Brundtland Commission report</strong></p>\n<p>The Brundtland Commission is the central historical starting point in your lecture.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><div><p>How many Millennium Development Goals preceded the SDGs?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 8</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 17</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 169</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p><strong>8</strong></p>\n<p>The MDGs contained eight goals, compared with 17 SDGs and 169 targets.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q7</span><div><p>Which combination correctly describes the UN Sustainable Development Goals?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 8 goals, 169 targets</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 17 goals, 17 targets</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 5 goals, 169 targets</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 17 goals, 169 targets</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p><strong>17 goals, 169 targets</strong></p>\n<p>The 2015 SDG agenda contains 17 goals and 169 targets.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q8</span><div><p>Which statement most accurately distinguishes the SDGs from ESG?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> SDGs and ESG are alternative names for the same rating system</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> SDGs are global goals; ESG concerns organisational environmental, social and governance factors</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> ESG contains 17 global goals, while SDGs contain three corporate ratings</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> SDGs apply only to government and cannot involve companies</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p><strong>SDGs are global goals; ESG concerns organisational environmental, social and governance factors</strong></p>\n<p>The SDGs specify global development ambitions; ESG is an organisational and investment-related lens.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q9</span><div><p>A company embeds workforce welfare, resource conservation and long-term financial viability into business strategy. This best demonstrates:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Corporate sustainability</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Greenwashing</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Financial reporting alone</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Pure regulatory compliance</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p><strong>Corporate sustainability</strong></p>\n<p>Corporate sustainability incorporates sustainability into business strategy and activities.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q10</span><div><p>Which initiative would most clearly represent substantive sustainability improvement rather than a purely promotional CSR activity?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Replacing the annual report cover with a forest photograph</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Issuing a new sustainability slogan</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Redesigning supplier practices to reduce verified labour and environmental impacts</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Donating a small amount while refusing to assess material operating risks</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p><strong>Redesigning supplier practices to reduce verified labour and environmental impacts</strong></p>\n<p>A real operational change addressing material impacts is stronger evidence of corporate sustainability than branding alone.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<h4>Section B - ESG classification and risk assessment</h4>\n<p>Questions 11-20 · Application and analysis</p>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q11</span><div><p>A plantation company's operations result in substantial deforestation. Which ESG dimension is most directly implicated?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Governance</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Social</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Environmental</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Financial accounting</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p><strong>Environmental</strong></p>\n<p>Deforestation and biodiversity are environmental factors.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q12</span><div><p>An electronics manufacturer is accused of requiring excessive overtime and unsafe working conditions. The primary ESG dimension is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Social</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Environmental</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Governance only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Market risk only</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p><strong>Social</strong></p>\n<p>Labour conditions and worker health and safety are Social factors.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q13</span><div><p>Shareholders question unusually high executive remuneration despite weak performance. Which ESG dimension is most directly involved?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Environmental</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Governance</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Social only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Climate risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p><strong>Governance</strong></p>\n<p>Executive compensation and board accountability fall under Governance.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q14</span><div><p>A technology company mishandles customers' personal information. Under the chapter's ESG classification, this is most directly associated with:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Deforestation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Energy consumption</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Executive remuneration</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Social issues involving privacy and data protection</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p><strong>Social issues involving privacy and data protection</strong></p>\n<p>The Social slide explicitly includes privacy and data protection.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q15</span><div><p>A factory pollutes a river, and directors intentionally conceal the event from regulators. Which analysis is strongest?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Only Social risk exists</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The problem is exclusively financial</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Environmental harm is compounded by governance failure</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> There is no ESG issue unless the share price declines</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p><strong>Environmental harm is compounded by governance failure</strong></p>\n<p>Pollution is Environmental; deliberate concealment adds Governance risk.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q16</span><div><p>A company's factory is flooded during extreme weather. The flood interrupts production and reduces revenue. Which chain best describes the situation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Governance → advertising → reputation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Credit risk → stock valuation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Employee diversity → market liquidity</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Environmental hazard → operational disruption → financial consequence</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p><strong>Environmental hazard → operational disruption → financial consequence</strong></p>\n<p>An environmental hazard can transmit into operational and financial risk.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q17</span><div><p>Which factor should be particularly important in deciding which ESG risks a company prioritises?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> How popular the topic is on social media</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Materiality to the company's activities and objectives</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Whether competitors use the same sustainability slogans</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Whether the issue appears first in an ESG checklist</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p><strong>Materiality to the company's activities and objectives</strong></p>\n<p>Materiality directs attention to ESG issues capable of significantly affecting operations, objectives or value.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q18</span><div><p>Two firms have identical environmental exposures. Company A has effective monitoring and controls, while Company B has weak controls. Which statement is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Their unmanaged risk may differ despite similar exposure</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Their ESG risks must be identical</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Company A has zero risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Company B necessarily has better sustainability performance</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p><strong>Their unmanaged risk may differ despite similar exposure</strong></p>\n<p>Exposure is only part of the risk picture; management effectiveness matters too.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q19</span><div><p>A listed company improves its ESG rating without meaningful evidence that its underlying operations improved. What is the best interpretation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Its sustainability is proven</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Its financial returns must rise</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Its operational risks have disappeared</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A better rating alone is insufficient evidence of substantive ESG improvement</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p><strong>A better rating alone is insufficient evidence of substantive ESG improvement</strong></p>\n<p>ESG ratings are indicators, not identical to sustainability performance.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q20</span><div><p>A Malaysian manufacturer's products face import restrictions after allegations of forced labour involving its operations or supply chain. Which sequence is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Governance → interest-rate risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Environmental pollution → insurance claims</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Social risk → market access and revenue consequences</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Pure financial risk without ESG relevance</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p><strong>Social risk → market access and revenue consequences</strong></p>\n<p>Labour practices are Social; trade restrictions can produce business and financial consequences.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<h4>Section C - Greenwashing, reporting and finance</h4>\n<p>Questions 21-30 · Application and evaluation</p>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q21</span><div><p>Which statement most clearly describes greenwashing?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Promoting unsupported or misleading environmental credentials</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Publishing an accurate report showing poor environmental performance</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Reducing carbon emissions through verified efficiency improvements</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Disclosing material environmental liabilities</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p><strong>Promoting unsupported or misleading environmental credentials</strong></p>\n<p>Greenwashing concerns misleading claims or impressions, not poor performance by itself.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q22</span><div><p>A company publicly admits that emissions increased and supplies transparent, verified evidence. What is the most accurate conclusion?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It is automatically greenwashing</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Poor environmental performance alone does not prove greenwashing</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It has achieved sustainable development</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Its ESG rating must improve</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p><strong>Poor environmental performance alone does not prove greenwashing</strong></p>\n<p>Truthful disclosure of weak results is different from misleading sustainability communication.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q23</span><div><p>A company claims all products are carbon-neutral but has no credible evidence supporting that claim. Which is the most immediate concern?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Credit risk only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A proven reduction in environmental risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Greenwashing and disclosure credibility</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A guaranteed increase in enterprise value</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p><strong>Greenwashing and disclosure credibility</strong></p>\n<p>Unsupported environmental claims create greenwashing and governance/reputational exposure.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q24</span><div><p>Management promises a 50% reduction in greenhouse-gas emissions but cannot determine its existing emissions. What should be prioritised?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Announcing a more ambitious target</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Publishing a promotional campaign</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Applying for ESG index inclusion</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Establishing a reliable emissions baseline</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p><strong>Establishing a reliable emissions baseline</strong></p>\n<p>Without a reliable baseline, progress against the reduction target cannot be measured credibly.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q25</span><div><p>Which distinction between ESG reporting and ESG risk management is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Reporting communicates ESG information; risk management identifies, assesses and responds to ESG risks</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Reporting automatically eliminates operational risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Risk management is mainly concerned with choosing report colours</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The two activities are unrelated</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p><strong>Reporting communicates ESG information; risk management identifies, assesses and responds to ESG risks</strong></p>\n<p>Good reporting supports transparency, while risk management involves substantive identification and response.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q26</span><div><p>Which pairing is most appropriate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> GRI → US corporate fraud legislation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> GRI → sustainability impact reporting; ISSB → sustainability-related financial disclosures</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> ISSB → only charitable donations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> ISO → FTSE4Good constituent selection</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p><strong>GRI → sustainability impact reporting; ISSB → sustainability-related financial disclosures</strong></p>\n<p>GRI and ISSB address different reporting perspectives, rather than being interchangeable instruments.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q27</span><div><p>Which organisation introduced international sustainability-disclosure standards discussed in the lecture?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> World Trade Organization</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Bank Negara Malaysia alone</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> International Sustainability Standards Board</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> FTSE Russell exclusively</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p><strong>International Sustainability Standards Board</strong></p>\n<p>The ISSB is highlighted in the lecture's sustainability-reporting standards discussion.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q28</span><div><p>A company issues financing to fund an eligible renewable-energy project, while an investment fund purchases shares in environmentally oriented businesses. Which distinction is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Project financing illustrates green finance; the fund's allocation illustrates green investing</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Both activities are necessarily greenwashing</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Only the share purchase is green finance</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Neither involves environmental considerations</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p><strong>Project financing illustrates green finance; the fund's allocation illustrates green investing</strong></p>\n<p>Green finance focuses on financing green activities; green investing takes the investor's allocation perspective.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q29</span><div><p>A manufacturer maps its emissions-reduction programme to SDG 13. Which statement is the most appropriate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It proves the company has achieved all 17 SDGs</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It guarantees FTSE4Good inclusion</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A single SDG mapping establishes sustainability across the business</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A relevant ESG initiative can contribute to an SDG, but the claimed contribution still requires evidence</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p><strong>A relevant ESG initiative can contribute to an SDG, but the claimed contribution still requires evidence</strong></p>\n<p>SDG alignment can show relevance, but labels alone do not verify results.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q30</span><div><p>What does net-zero greenhouse-gas emissions mean in the chapter?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> No company may produce any emissions under any circumstances</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Emissions produced are balanced by greenhouse-gas removals</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Emissions must grow at the same rate as profit</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Carbon reporting becomes unnecessary</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p><strong>Emissions produced are balanced by greenhouse-gas removals</strong></p>\n<p>Net zero concerns a balance between emissions produced and removals, not necessarily zero gross emissions.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<h4>Section D - Malaysia and lecture case studies</h4>\n<p>Questions 31-39 · Source-specific application</p>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q31</span><div><p>According to the lecture, FTSE4Good Bursa Malaysia was launched in December 2014 and draws its constituents from:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> All Malaysian private businesses</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Only Malaysian government-linked banks</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The FTSE Bursa Malaysia EMAS index universe</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Only companies issuing green sukuk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p><strong>The FTSE Bursa Malaysia EMAS index universe</strong></p>\n<p>The lecture identifies the FBM EMAS index as the constituent universe and December 2014 as the launch date.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q32</span><div><p>A manufacturer wants FTSE4Good inclusion. Which strategy is most consistent with meaningful ESG risk management?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Focus entirely on publicity</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Purchase more ESG advertising</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Ignore suppliers and concentrate on the index score</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Identify material ESG exposures, improve controls, measure outcomes and then pursue index eligibility</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p><strong>Identify material ESG exposures, improve controls, measure outcomes and then pursue index eligibility</strong></p>\n<p>Index inclusion should follow credible performance, not substitute for it.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q33</span><div><p>Which PETRONAS-related ESG issue does the lecture highlight for 2021?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Exclusion from certain JPMorgan ESG-related indices</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A major US food-safety prosecution</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> An employee stock-option controversy only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A ban on all PETRONAS products</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p><strong>Exclusion from certain JPMorgan ESG-related indices</strong></p>\n<p>The lecture uses PETRONAS as an example of ESG-index exclusion. It does not establish the other choices.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q34</span><div><p>Which underlying ESG factor is central to the Malaysian glove and palm-oil cases involving US import restrictions?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Board gender diversity</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Forced-labour allegations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Water pricing only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Corporate dividend policy</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p><strong>Forced-labour allegations</strong></p>\n<p>The slides connect these restrictions with labour-related allegations, an S factor.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q35</span><div><p>The Sapura Energy example primarily highlights concerns about:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Biodiversity</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Pollution</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Executive remuneration and governance</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Renewable electricity generation</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p><strong>Executive remuneration and governance</strong></p>\n<p>The lecture links the case with objections to excessive executive compensation.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q36</span><div><p>The Serba Dinamik case in the lecture most directly illustrates ESG concerns involving:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Climate adaptation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Employee healthcare access</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Water scarcity</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Financial-reporting integrity and governance</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p><strong>Financial-reporting integrity and governance</strong></p>\n<p>Financial-statement issues concern the reliability of reporting and company governance.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q37</span><div><p>The Tesla ESG-index exclusion example in the lecture highlights concerns involving working conditions, discrimination and safety-related investigations. Which is the best interpretation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Social and governance concerns can affect ESG-index decisions even for a company selling electric vehicles</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Electric vehicles automatically guarantee high ESG ratings</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Environmental products eliminate all Social risks</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Only carbon emissions matter in an ESG index</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p><strong>Social and governance concerns can affect ESG-index decisions even for a company selling electric vehicles</strong></p>\n<p>An environmentally oriented product does not eliminate Social or Governance concerns.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q38</span><div><p>The Tlou Energy case is used in the lecture to illustrate:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Successful elimination of all emissions</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Regulatory consequences of incorrect environmental claims</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Only conventional credit risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Compulsory FTSE4Good membership</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p><strong>Regulatory consequences of incorrect environmental claims</strong></p>\n<p>The case involves Australian regulatory action concerning incorrect environmental claims.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q39</span><div><p>The Coca-Cola case in the lecture primarily raises questions about:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> How to calculate bond duration</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The legality of dividend payments</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Whether environmental marketing claims are consistent with plastic-waste impacts</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Bank Negara's monetary policy</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p><strong>Whether environmental marketing claims are consistent with plastic-waste impacts</strong></p>\n<p>The slide uses allegations and criticism surrounding sustainability marketing and plastic waste to illustrate greenwashing risk.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<h4>Section E - Higher-order ERM scenarios</h4>\n<p>Questions 40-45 · Analyse, evaluate and create</p>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q40</span><div><p>A factory has repeated near-miss safety incidents but has not yet experienced a serious accident. Which would be the most appropriate ESG early-warning indicator?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Number of trees in its annual report</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Total market capitalisation alone</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Number of sustainability slogans</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Near-miss frequency and severity trends</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p><strong>Near-miss frequency and severity trends</strong></p>\n<p>Near-miss trends can act as operational Social-risk warning indicators before a severe incident.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q41</span><div><p>A manufacturing board has limited ESG resources. Which action should come first?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Identify its material ESG exposures and objectives</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Publish the longest possible report</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Copy all ESG targets used by an unrelated bank</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Allocate every ESG issue the same priority</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p><strong>Identify its material ESG exposures and objectives</strong></p>\n<p>Materiality and objectives guide which issues deserve resources and controls.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q42</span><div><p>A company lacks supplier assessments, emissions data and board oversight but wants sustainability financing. Which sequence is most credible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Apply for financing → announce success → consider data later</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Establish oversight → identify material risks → collect baselines → introduce controls → report outcomes</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Buy an ESG rating → declare compliance → avoid supplier assessment</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Publish a glossy report → assume risk management is complete</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p><strong>Establish oversight → identify material risks → collect baselines → introduce controls → report outcomes</strong></p>\n<p>Substantive governance, measurement and operational action should precede claims of ESG achievement.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q43</span><div><p>A CEO's bonus depends only on short-term profit, even though safety and environmental incidents are increasing. What is the strongest diagnosis?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Financial incentives guarantee sustainable performance</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The problem is only with the marketing department</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Governance incentives may undermine environmental and social objectives</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> There is no issue because profit is increasing</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p><strong>Governance incentives may undermine environmental and social objectives</strong></p>\n<p>Incentive design is a Governance concern and can directly influence E and S outcomes.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q44</span><div><p>An industrial company's emissions harm surrounding communities. New regulation then raises its operating costs. Which interpretation best reflects ESG risk management?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Only environmental impact matters; financial consequences are irrelevant</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> ESG exists solely to maximise ratings</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Regulatory costs prove that sustainability is unrelated to enterprise value</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The company's environmental impact and the resulting financial exposure are interconnected</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p><strong>The company's environmental impact and the resulting financial exposure are interconnected</strong></p>\n<p>An outward environmental impact can generate inward operational, regulatory and financial risk.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q45</span><div><p>A company's verified environmental investment costs RM20 million and is expected to reduce operating costs, regulatory exposure and future disruption. Which approach is most consistent with ERM?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Evaluate the investment's costs, expected benefits, uncertainty and long-term enterprise value</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Reject it because sustainability costs money</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Approve it solely to generate positive publicity</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Assume every green investment produces a guaranteed financial return</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p><strong>Evaluate the investment's costs, expected benefits, uncertainty and long-term enterprise value</strong></p>\n<p>ERM evaluates the trade-off between costs, risk reduction, opportunities and long-term value, rather than blindly accepting or rejecting a project.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4p-s15\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Mock exam - answers and reasoning</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the answer key</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><hr />\n<h3>Q1 - C</h3>\n<p><strong>Meeting current needs without compromising future generations' needs</strong></p>\n<p>The defining feature is intergenerational responsibility combined with economic, social and environmental balance.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q2 - B</h3>\n<p><strong>Current performance undermines future capacity</strong></p>\n<p>Sustainability asks whether present activities compromise future needs, not whether current profit is positive.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q3 - D</h3>\n<p><strong>People, Planet, Profit</strong></p>\n<p>The Triple Bottom Line combines social, environmental and economic dimensions.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q4 - A</h3>\n<p><strong>Performance</strong></p>\n<p>The 5Ps are People, Planet, Prosperity, Peace and Partnership.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q5 - C</h3>\n<p><strong>The 1987 Brundtland Commission report</strong></p>\n<p>The Brundtland Commission is the central historical starting point in your lecture.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q6 - B</h3>\n<p><strong>8</strong></p>\n<p>The MDGs contained eight goals, compared with 17 SDGs and 169 targets.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q7 - D</h3>\n<p><strong>17 goals, 169 targets</strong></p>\n<p>The 2015 SDG agenda contains 17 goals and 169 targets.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q8 - B</h3>\n<p><strong>SDGs are global goals; ESG concerns organisational environmental, social and governance factors</strong></p>\n<p>The SDGs specify global development ambitions; ESG is an organisational and investment-related lens.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q9 - A</h3>\n<p><strong>Corporate sustainability</strong></p>\n<p>Corporate sustainability incorporates sustainability into business strategy and activities.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q10 - C</h3>\n<p><strong>Redesigning supplier practices to reduce verified labour and environmental impacts</strong></p>\n<p>A real operational change addressing material impacts is stronger evidence of corporate sustainability than branding alone.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q11 - C</h3>\n<p><strong>Environmental</strong></p>\n<p>Deforestation and biodiversity are environmental factors.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q12 - A</h3>\n<p><strong>Social</strong></p>\n<p>Labour conditions and worker health and safety are Social factors.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q13 - B</h3>\n<p><strong>Governance</strong></p>\n<p>Executive compensation and board accountability fall under Governance.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q14 - D</h3>\n<p><strong>Social issues involving privacy and data protection</strong></p>\n<p>The Social slide explicitly includes privacy and data protection.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q15 - C</h3>\n<p><strong>Environmental harm is compounded by governance failure</strong></p>\n<p>Pollution is Environmental; deliberate concealment adds Governance risk.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q16 - D</h3>\n<p><strong>Environmental hazard → operational disruption → financial consequence</strong></p>\n<p>An environmental hazard can transmit into operational and financial risk.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q17 - B</h3>\n<p><strong>Materiality to the company's activities and objectives</strong></p>\n<p>Materiality directs attention to ESG issues capable of significantly affecting operations, objectives or value.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q18 - A</h3>\n<p><strong>Their unmanaged risk may differ despite similar exposure</strong></p>\n<p>Exposure is only part of the risk picture; management effectiveness matters too.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q19 - D</h3>\n<p><strong>A better rating alone is insufficient evidence of substantive ESG improvement</strong></p>\n<p>ESG ratings are indicators, not identical to sustainability performance.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q20 - C</h3>\n<p><strong>Social risk → market access and revenue consequences</strong></p>\n<p>Labour practices are Social; trade restrictions can produce business and financial consequences.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q21 - A</h3>\n<p><strong>Promoting unsupported or misleading environmental credentials</strong></p>\n<p>Greenwashing concerns misleading claims or impressions, not poor performance by itself.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q22 - B</h3>\n<p><strong>Poor environmental performance alone does not prove greenwashing</strong></p>\n<p>Truthful disclosure of weak results is different from misleading sustainability communication.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q23 - C</h3>\n<p><strong>Greenwashing and disclosure credibility</strong></p>\n<p>Unsupported environmental claims create greenwashing and governance/reputational exposure.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q24 - D</h3>\n<p><strong>Establishing a reliable emissions baseline</strong></p>\n<p>Without a reliable baseline, progress against the reduction target cannot be measured credibly.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q25 - A</h3>\n<p><strong>Reporting communicates ESG information; risk management identifies, assesses and responds to ESG risks</strong></p>\n<p>Good reporting supports transparency, while risk management involves substantive identification and response.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q26 - B</h3>\n<p><strong>GRI → sustainability impact reporting; ISSB → sustainability-related financial disclosures</strong></p>\n<p>GRI and ISSB address different reporting perspectives, rather than being interchangeable instruments.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q27 - C</h3>\n<p><strong>International Sustainability Standards Board</strong></p>\n<p>The ISSB is highlighted in the lecture's sustainability-reporting standards discussion.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q28 - A</h3>\n<p><strong>Project financing illustrates green finance; the fund's allocation illustrates green investing</strong></p>\n<p>Green finance focuses on financing green activities; green investing takes the investor's allocation perspective.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q29 - D</h3>\n<p><strong>A relevant ESG initiative can contribute to an SDG, but the claimed contribution still requires evidence</strong></p>\n<p>SDG alignment can show relevance, but labels alone do not verify results.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q30 - B</h3>\n<p><strong>Emissions produced are balanced by greenhouse-gas removals</strong></p>\n<p>Net zero concerns a balance between emissions produced and removals, not necessarily zero gross emissions.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q31 - C</h3>\n<p><strong>The FTSE Bursa Malaysia EMAS index universe</strong></p>\n<p>The lecture identifies the FBM EMAS index as the constituent universe and December 2014 as the launch date.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q32 - D</h3>\n<p><strong>Identify material ESG exposures, improve controls, measure outcomes and then pursue index eligibility</strong></p>\n<p>Index inclusion should follow credible performance, not substitute for it.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q33 - A</h3>\n<p><strong>Exclusion from certain JPMorgan ESG-related indices</strong></p>\n<p>The lecture uses PETRONAS as an example of ESG-index exclusion. It does not establish the other choices.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q34 - B</h3>\n<p><strong>Forced-labour allegations</strong></p>\n<p>The slides connect these restrictions with labour-related allegations, an S factor.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q35 - C</h3>\n<p><strong>Executive remuneration and governance</strong></p>\n<p>The lecture links the case with objections to excessive executive compensation.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q36 - D</h3>\n<p><strong>Financial-reporting integrity and governance</strong></p>\n<p>Financial-statement issues concern the reliability of reporting and company governance.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q37 - A</h3>\n<p><strong>Social and governance concerns can affect ESG-index decisions even for a company selling electric vehicles</strong></p>\n<p>An environmentally oriented product does not eliminate Social or Governance concerns.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q38 - B</h3>\n<p><strong>Regulatory consequences of incorrect environmental claims</strong></p>\n<p>The case involves Australian regulatory action concerning incorrect environmental claims.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q39 - C</h3>\n<p><strong>Whether environmental marketing claims are consistent with plastic-waste impacts</strong></p>\n<p>The slide uses allegations and criticism surrounding sustainability marketing and plastic waste to illustrate greenwashing risk.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q40 - D</h3>\n<p><strong>Near-miss frequency and severity trends</strong></p>\n<p>Near-miss trends can act as operational Social-risk warning indicators before a severe incident.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q41 - A</h3>\n<p><strong>Identify its material ESG exposures and objectives</strong></p>\n<p>Materiality and objectives guide which issues deserve resources and controls.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q42 - B</h3>\n<p><strong>Establish oversight → identify material risks → collect baselines → introduce controls → report outcomes</strong></p>\n<p>Substantive governance, measurement and operational action should precede claims of ESG achievement.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q43 - C</h3>\n<p><strong>Governance incentives may undermine environmental and social objectives</strong></p>\n<p>Incentive design is a Governance concern and can directly influence E and S outcomes.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q44 - D</h3>\n<p><strong>The company's environmental impact and the resulting financial exposure are interconnected</strong></p>\n<p>An outward environmental impact can generate inward operational, regulatory and financial risk.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q45 - A</h3>\n<p><strong>Evaluate the investment's costs, expected benefits, uncertainty and long-term enterprise value</strong></p>\n<p>ERM evaluates the trade-off between costs, risk reduction, opportunities and long-term value, rather than blindly accepting or rejecting a project.</p>\n<hr /></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"t4p-d16\">PART D - DIFFICULT QUESTIONS &amp; FINAL REVIEW</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4p-s17\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 12 - How to think through difficult questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t4_goodhart\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Optimising the rating can raise the score while real performance barely moves.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The questions above include straightforward factual recall, but the final section is deliberately more demanding. These are the types of reasoning you should develop.</p>\n<h3>Scenario A - Environmental or governance risk?</h3>\n<p>A company releases toxic waste into a river, but directors deliberately conceal the incident.</p>\n<p>You see two choices:</p>\n<p>A. Environmental risk B. Governance risk</p>\n<p>Both can be defensible descriptions.</p>\n<p>But the correct answer depends on the wording.</p>\n<p>If the question asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What ESG issue arises from releasing toxic waste?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Choose Environmental.</p>\n<p>If it asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What ESG weakness is demonstrated by directors concealing the incident?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Choose Governance.</p>\n<p>If it asks which dimensions are involved overall, both may be correct.</p>\n<p>Exam rule: Distinguish the harmful activity from the management failure.</p>\n<h3>Scenario B - ESG risk or financial risk?</h3>\n<p>A company's supplier is accused of forced labour. Foreign authorities block shipments, causing revenue to decline.</p>\n<p>The chain is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Social Risk}} \\rightarrow \\boxed{\\text{Trade Restriction}} \\rightarrow \\boxed{\\text{Financial Consequence}}$$</div>\n<p>If asked for the underlying ESG issue, choose Social.</p>\n<p>If asked for the economic consequence, select loss of sales, market access or profitability.</p>\n<h3>Scenario C - Greenwashing or weak ESG performance?</h3>\n<p>Company A admits that its carbon emissions increased by 20% and explains the reason in a reliable report.</p>\n<p>Company B claims carbon neutrality but has no substantiation.</p>\n<p>Which is greenwashing?</p>\n<p>Company B is the clearer example.</p>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>Because:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Poor Performance} \\neq \\text{Misleading Claims}}$$</div>\n<p>Do not punish transparency by automatically labelling every negative result greenwashing.</p>\n<h3>Scenario D - ESG score or sustainable value?</h3>\n<p>A company's ESG rating improves from 60 to 85.</p>\n<p>Can we conclude that the company has become substantially more sustainable?</p>\n<p>Not from that information alone.</p>\n<p>We would need to understand:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>What the rating measures.</li>\n<li>Whether its methodology changed.</li>\n<li>Whether the company's underlying performance improved.</li>\n<li>Whether the reported data are reliable.</li>\n<li>Whether material ESG risks are better controlled.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Remember:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>A measurement is evidence, not the entire reality.</span></div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4p-s18\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 13 - What to memorise the night before</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Topic 4 memory sheet</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sustainability</th>\n<th>Present + future generations</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Triple Bottom Line</td>\n<td>People + Planet + Profit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SDGs</td>\n<td>17 goals, 169 targets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SDG 5Ps</td>\n<td>People, Planet, Prosperity, Peace, Partnership</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Environmental</td>\n<td>Energy, emissions, pollution, water, biodiversity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Social</td>\n<td>Workers, safety, labour, diversity, communities, privacy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Governance</td>\n<td>Board, oversight, corruption, executive compensation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ESG Risk</td>\n<td>Material E/S/G events affecting value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Greenwashing</td>\n<td>Misleading environmental claims</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ESG Metrics</td>\n<td>Measures of performance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ESG Reporting</td>\n<td>Disclosure, transparency and information</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ESG Risk Management</td>\n<td>Identify → Assess → Respond → Monitor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Green Finance</td>\n<td>Financing environmentally beneficial activities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Green Investing</td>\n<td>Allocating capital to environmentally oriented investments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FTSE4Good Bursa Malaysia</td>\n<td>ESG-related equity index</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4p-s19\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 14 - Final exam strategy</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>For Topic 4, I would prioritise your revision in this order:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>High priority: Sustainability vs ESG vs SDGs; E/S/G classification; greenwashing; ESG risk identification; materiality.</li>\n<li>High priority: Linking ESG causes to financial, operational, reputational and regulatory consequences.</li>\n<li>Medium-high priority: Triple Bottom Line, 5Ps, reporting standards, green financing and ESG metrics.</li>\n<li>Medium priority: Malaysian institutions, FTSE4Good, company examples, sustainability timeline and selected historical dates.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For a technical ERM MCQ, the strongest answer is often the option that connects the underlying ESG issue to the company's objectives and risk-management response, rather than the one that merely sounds most environmentally friendly.</p>\n<p>The central idea connecting all four topics is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"ESG Issue → Risk Exposure → Business Consequence → Risk Control → Long-term Enterprise Value\"><span>ESG Issue</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Risk Exposure</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Business Consequence</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Risk Control</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Long-term Enterprise Value</span></div>\n<p>Your target should be at least 40/45 (approximately 89%) on the practice test, with particular attention to the scenario questions. Getting definitions right is necessary; distinguishing root causes, consequences, materiality and management responses is what demonstrates deeper understanding.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"question-bank-wrap hard-wrap\" id=\"t4-hard\"><summary><div><strong>Difficult mode - 30 questions</strong><small>18 Bloom-graded questions (3 per level) and 12 marked extension questions. Answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><p class=\"hard-intro\">Questions climb Bloom&#39;s taxonomy from <b>Remember</b> to <b>Create</b>. The <b>extension</b> questions go beyond the slides (industry practice, auditors, boards, Master&#39;s, PhD and postdoc level) but can be reasoned out from the topic&#39;s principles.</p><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn\" data-quiz=\"4\" data-quiz-mode=\"difficult\">Start Topic 4 difficult mode</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"4\" data-quiz-mode=\"extensions\">Extensions only</button></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>How many goals and targets make up the 2015 UN Sustainable Development Goals?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 8 goals, 21 targets</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 17 goals, 169 targets</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 15 goals, 100 targets</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 5 goals, 17 targets</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The 2030 Agenda has <strong>17 goals and 169 targets</strong>, organised around the 5Ps. The 2000 MDGs had 8 goals.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>The Triple Bottom Line consists of:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> People, planet, prosperity</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> People, planet, partnership</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> People, planet, profit</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Profit, prosperity, peace</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>John Elkington's TBL = people, planet, profit. Prosperity, peace and partnership belong to the SDG 5Ps.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>When was the FTSE4Good Bursa Malaysia Index launched, and from which universe are constituents drawn?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> December 2014; FTSE Bursa Malaysia EMAS</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 2021; FBM KLCI only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 2004; all ASEAN exchanges</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 2015; government-linked companies only</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The slide: launched December 2014 as the local exchange's ESG measure, drawing constituents from the FTSE Bursa Malaysia EMAS index.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Why is sustainability <strong>not</strong> the same as environmentalism?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because sustainability ignores the environment</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because environmentalism is a legal requirement</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because sustainability only concerns profit</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because sustainability balances economic, environmental and social needs over time; a green but unviable firm, or a profitable firm using forced labour, is not sustainable</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Sustainability is a three-way balance across generations, not just environmental protection.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Which best distinguishes SDGs from ESG?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> SDGs are global development goals; ESG concerns an organisation's environmental, social and governance factors</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> They are alternative names for the same thing</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> ESG contains 17 goals</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> SDGs apply only to companies</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The SDGs answer \"what outcomes should the world pursue?\"; ESG answers \"how is this organisation performing on or exposed to E, S and G?\". ESG actions can contribute to SDGs.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>What makes an ESG issue an <strong>ESG risk</strong> in the slides' sense?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Any ESG topic mentioned by the media</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It must be environmental</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It is an E, S or G event or condition that could have a material negative impact on value</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It must already have caused a loss</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The key word is <strong>material</strong>: capable of meaningfully affecting value. It need not have occurred, and it can be E, S or G.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>A company reports \"tonnes of CO₂e emitted per year\". This is best classified as:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> An SDG</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> An ESG metric</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> ESG risk management</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Greenwashing</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>It measures environmental performance: an ESG metric. SDG 13 is the global goal it may relate to.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>A bank provides a loan to build a solar farm; separately, a fund buys shares in renewable-energy firms. Classify each.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Both green investing</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Both green finance</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Green investing; green finance</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Green finance; green investing</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Funding an environmental activity is <strong>green finance</strong>; an investor allocating capital to environmentally oriented firms is <strong>green investing</strong>.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>Products from Malaysian glove and palm-oil firms were withheld by US Customs over forced-labour allegations. Which ESG pillar is the root issue?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Social</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Environmental</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Governance</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> None; it is purely a trade issue</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Forced labour is a labour-standards (S) issue; the trade restriction and lost revenue are its consequences.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>A company's marketing claims a product is \"carbon neutral\", but nobody verified the data. Why is this as much a governance problem as an environmental one?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because emissions are not environmental</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because governance concerns only board size</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because the failure lies in who approved the claim, whether evidence was checked and whether controls existed</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because carbon neutrality is illegal</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Unverified claims reveal weak approval, verification and accountability: G questions. Consequences then spread to reputation, legal and financial risk.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>Company A faces high ESG exposure but manages it well; Company B faces moderate exposure with almost no controls. What can you conclude?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> B is safer because exposure is lower</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A is riskier in every case</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Exposure and management quality are different; B may carry more unmanaged risk despite lower exposure</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Both are equally risky</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The ASEAN comparison slide separates exposure from the ability to manage it. Assess both.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>Trace the most likely chain from drought to a financial problem for a manufacturer.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Drought → water shortage → production declines → revenue declines → cash flow deteriorates → liquidity pressure</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Drought → higher share price → more investment</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Drought → lower taxes → profit rises</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Drought → greenwashing → fines</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>An environmental condition affecting the company becomes operational, then business, then liquidity risk: environmental risk becomes conventional financial risk.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D13</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>Management sets the objective \"achieve an ESG score of 90\". The best evaluation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Ideal, because scores measure sustainability exactly</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Risky: optimising the score can replace real improvement; the objective should be managing material risks and creating long-term value, with the score as an outcome</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Irrelevant, because scores have no effect</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Acceptable only for banks</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Thermometer ≠ health. Good ESG performance → rating, not rating → pretend performance.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D14</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>A miner reports its high emissions accurately and sets a modest, verified reduction target. Is this greenwashing?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Yes, because emissions are high</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Yes, because the target is modest</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> No: weak performance disclosed honestly is not greenwashing, which requires claims exceeding reality</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> No, because mining is exempt</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Greenwashing is about misleading or unsupported communication, not poor performance itself.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D15</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>A food manufacturer pledges carbon neutrality but lacks an emissions baseline, has a supplier accused of forced labour, no board ESG oversight and EBITDA-only bonuses. Best judgement?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Credible, because the pledge is public</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Not credible yet: no baseline, social supply-chain exposure and weak governance mean the claim exceeds evidence, creating greenwashing risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Irrelevant to ERM</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Credible if marketing approves it</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Evaluate against baseline, measurability, pathway, ownership, investment, supplier inclusion, consequences and reporting. Here governance, data and incentives are all missing.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D16</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>Which order should a manufacturer follow to seek FTSE4Good inclusion and sustainable financing credibly?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Publish a report → buy a rating → assess suppliers later</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Set targets → publish → establish governance</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Marketing agency → green photos → 80-page PDF</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Governance → materiality → data → baseline → targets → controls → performance → disclosure</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Governance and data come before reporting; otherwise the firm wanders into greenwashing. Index inclusion should follow as an outcome.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D17</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>Which set of KRIs best fits a manufacturer's material ESG risks?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Share price and dividend yield only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Energy intensity and GHG emissions; injury rate and supplier labour violations; control breaches and ESG issues escalated to the board</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Number of sustainability photos in the annual report</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The ESG rating only</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>KRIs should be early-warning indicators tied to material exposures across E, S and G, not outcome scores or cosmetic counts.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D18</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>How should ESG risk management be positioned inside the organisation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> As a separate department with its own independent risk process</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Within marketing</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Inside ERM: material ESG issues are drivers feeding strategic, operational, market, reputation, compliance and liquidity risk processes</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Outsourced entirely to rating agencies</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>ERM ⊃ material ESG risks. ESG provides drivers and indicators for existing processes; Topic 4 extends ERM rather than replacing it.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension questions</span><small>beyond the notes, lowest Bloom level first</small></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>Why does \"net zero\" not mean \"zero emissions\"?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Net zero counts only Scope 1 emissions</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Net zero balances emissions produced with emissions removed, so gross emissions can remain above zero</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Net zero applies only to governments</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> They mean the same thing</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Gross emissions - removals = net emissions. Malaysia's 2050 carbon-neutrality target (announced September 2021) is a net concept.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>For a semiconductor fab, which ESG issue is most likely highly material?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Water availability</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Lobbying disclosures only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Office paper use</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Staff canteen menus</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Fabs are water-intensive; shortages can halt production. Materiality is business-specific.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Auditor</span></div><p>An auditor reviews a reported 100,000 tCO₂e figure. Which question is most important?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Is the report well designed?</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> How many photos are included?</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Was the figure collected, bounded (including subsidiaries) and reviewed under controls that prevent manipulation?</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Did marketing approve it?</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>ESG reporting quality = measurement + controls + governance, not graphic design.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>PETRONAS was excluded from certain JP Morgan ESG indices in 2021 due to a low ESG score. Which transmission mechanism does this illustrate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> S → trade restriction → revenue</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> ESG performance → index eligibility → capital-market implications</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> G → executive pay → shareholder vote</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> E → flood → factory damage</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Index exclusion affects which investors can hold the securities, linking ESG performance to access to capital.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>Why can capital markets change corporate ESG behaviour?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because investors ignore ESG</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because ESG has no effect on cost of capital</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because firms with unmanaged ESG liabilities may face higher required returns or exclusion, while credible practices can widen access to sustainable financing</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because ratings replace regulation</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Financial incentives → corporate ESG behaviour; this is why ESG moved onto CFO and board agendas and why sustainable bonds and sukuk grew.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>\"ESG problems rarely remain ESG problems.\" Which example best illustrates the G pathway?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Drought → water shortage → output falls</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Forced labour → import ban → lost revenue</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Falsified financial statements → regulatory charges → share sell-down (Serba Dinamik)</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Flood → factory damage</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>G → reporting credibility → regulatory, reputational and market consequences. The other chains start from E or S.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Auditor</span></div><p>A company produces an excellent sustainability report but has weak internal sustainability controls. The auditor's conclusion?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Strong ESG risk management, because reporting is excellent</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Reporting and risk management are related but not identical; weak controls mean the reported information and risk management are both unreliable</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> No issue, because reports are voluntary</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The report should be shortened</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Reporting asks \"what do we disclose?\"; risk management asks \"what do we do?\". Reliable data comes from managed risks and controls.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Board</span></div><p>A board wants to copy PETRONAS's ESG strategy for its bank. The best advice?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Copy it exactly; PETRONAS is a leader</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Adopt all 17 SDGs equally</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Tailor ESG to the bank's own material exposures, such as governance, data privacy and financed activities</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Focus only on office energy use</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>ESG strategy should fit actual exposure, as IOI does by mapping only relevant SDGs, echoing ISO 31000's \"tailored\" principle.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>Critique: \"An ESG index inclusion proves a firm is sustainable.\"</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Valid: indices measure sustainability directly</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Valid for Malaysian firms only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Invalid: an index is a classification or filter based on defined criteria; it is an outcome signal, not sustainability itself</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Invalid, because indices are illegal</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Index = classification/benchmark/filter. Treating it as the goal invites metric optimisation over real risk reduction.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>Should a firm prioritise all 120 ESG indicators it can find equally?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Yes, completeness is always best</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Yes, regulators require it</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> No, ESG should be ignored</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> No: materiality focuses resources on the factors capable of meaningfully affecting objectives and value</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Without materiality, ESG becomes an infinite checklist. Risk management requires prioritisation.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Board</span></div><p>Which board mechanism most reduces greenwashing risk?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A larger marketing budget</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Board oversight of sustainability claims: data ownership, pre-publication verification and accountability for disclosures</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> More ESG awards</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Removing all sustainability disclosure</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Greenwashing is largely a governance failure; verification and accountability close the claim-reality gap. Silence forgoes legitimate value and may breach reporting expectations.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>Design an ESG risk programme for a listed company. Which architecture fits Topic 4 best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Business objectives → material ESG issues → risk assessment → governance and owners → baselines, metrics and KRIs → targets, controls, responses → monitoring → disclosure → review</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Disclosure → rating → targets → governance</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Ratings agency → marketing → report</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Targets only, without baselines</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>This is the create-level architecture in the notes: governance and data before disclosure, with continuous review. Ratings should follow, not lead.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div></details>");
